1
00:00:00,380 --> 00:00:01,240
[1949 film] One couple.

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:05,160
[1949 film] That means a date.

3
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:08,020
[1949 film] Not like just going around with the crowd.

4
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:10,780
[1949 film] Just me and the girl.

5
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:13,880
[1949 film] Well, that's all right.

6
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:17,560
[1949 film] Only... what girl?

7
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:19,200
[1949 film] Who?

8
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:24,000
In 1949, Coronet Instructional Films of Glenview,

9
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:27,260
Illinois, shot this color film for American high school

10
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:31,340
classrooms. Its subject was not algebra or hygiene.

11
00:00:31,420 --> 00:00:33,180
It was how to go on a date.

12
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:35,600
So what exactly were teenagers being taught,

13
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:38,540
and why were adults so anxious to teach it?

14
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:42,120
[1949 film] How do you choose a date?

15
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:44,560
[1949 film] Whose company would you enjoy?

16
00:00:45,860 --> 00:00:48,160
[1949 film] Well, one thing you can consider is looks.

17
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:51,880
[1949 film] What about Ann?

18
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:56,500
[1949 film] She knows how to have a good time, and how to make the fellow with her relax.

19
00:00:56,740 --> 00:00:57,520
[1949 film] Have fun, too.

20
00:00:59,060 --> 00:01:00,900
[1949 film] Yes, that's what a boy likes.

21
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:03,080
[1949 film] He wants to know he's appreciated.

22
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:05,460
[1949 film] Ann would be fun on a date.

23
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:10,060
Notice the rule. A girl is measured by how she makes a

24
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:13,900
boy feel: appreciated, relaxed, never inferior.

25
00:01:13,980 --> 00:01:15,520
That was no accident.

26
00:01:15,820 --> 00:01:18,860
The film's educational collaborator was Reuben Hill,

27
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:21,520
a family sociologist at the University of North

28
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:24,800
Carolina, and after the war Coronet produced scores of

29
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:26,700
these social guidance films.

30
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:29,900
Historian Ken Smith calls them a postwar morality

31
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:32,740
offensive by educators, psychologists,

32
00:01:32,780 --> 00:01:33,720
and the clergy.

33
00:01:35,180 --> 00:01:37,240
[1949 film] Ann? This is Woody.

34
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:42,780
[1949 film] Well, I have a ticket for the Hi-Teen Carnival Saturday, and...

35
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:43,740
[1949 film] Well, would you like to go?

36
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:46,160
[1949 film] Why, yes, Woody.

37
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:48,480
[1949 film] I'll have to talk to my folks about it.

38
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:51,480
[1949 film] But I think I can go. That'll be fine.

39
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:55,260
Every wrong turn is a lesson in a code.

40
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:58,280
The boy initiates, offers a concrete plan,

41
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:00,660
names a time, and her parents hold a veto.

42
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:03,980
Historian Beth Bailey has shown how the prewar ideal of

43
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:06,700
popularity, measured by how many different people you

44
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:09,720
dated, gave way after the war to pairing off and going

45
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:12,260
steady. The stakes were real.

46
00:02:12,440 --> 00:02:15,920
In 1950 the median American bride was twenty,

47
00:02:15,960 --> 00:02:18,620
the groom twenty-two, among the youngest the Census

48
00:02:18,660 --> 00:02:20,520
Bureau has ever recorded.

49
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:25,140
[1949 film] Well, if you don't overdo on dating...

50
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:29,440
[1949 film] Ed knows what I mean. Weekends only, and not too late.

51
00:02:29,980 --> 00:02:33,600
[1949 film] I think the important thing about a date is to have a good time.

52
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:36,540
[1949 film] You don't need to spend a lot of money to do that.

53
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:43,080
[1949 film] You just enjoy whatever you do, whether it's movies or parties or anything.

54
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:46,560
[1949 film] And you leave your boyfriend enough money, so he'll ask you again.

55
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:50,940
[1949 film] Any girl who can't be ready on time for a date isn't good enough for my boy.

56
00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:54,480
The economics are just as explicit.

57
00:02:54,580 --> 00:02:57,400
He pays. She protects his wallet, so there will be a

58
00:02:57,440 --> 00:02:59,620
second date. Her parents set the curfew,

59
00:02:59,660 --> 00:03:01,640
and his mother sets the schedule.

60
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:03,740
The audience for all this was new.

61
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:06,800
The word teenager only entered everyday American speech

62
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:10,440
in the 1940s, pushed by advertisers and by magazines

63
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:13,680
like Seventeen, founded in 1944.

64
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:16,760
Adults were writing rules for a generation with spending

65
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:20,220
money of its own, and evenings far from the front porch.

66
00:03:21,630 --> 00:03:24,070
[1949 film] But how do you say good night?

67
00:03:26,770 --> 00:03:27,210
[1949 film] Perhaps...

68
00:03:29,290 --> 00:03:30,450
[1949 film] Don't leave!

69
00:03:33,790 --> 00:03:35,210
[1949 film] 1 a.m.

70
00:03:36,010 --> 00:03:38,190
[1949 film] Well, it's getting late.

71
00:03:38,670 --> 00:03:39,590
[1949 film] Yes, it is.

72
00:03:40,150 --> 00:03:42,330
[1949 film] I'd ask you in for a bite to eat if it weren't so late.

73
00:03:43,850 --> 00:03:47,270
[1949 film] Let's plan to get home in time for a sandwich or something next time.

74
00:03:47,750 --> 00:03:49,190
[1949 film] Say, that sounds good.

75
00:03:49,650 --> 00:03:50,710
[1949 film] I'll call you next week.

76
00:03:51,030 --> 00:03:51,470
[1949 film] Will you?

77
00:03:52,270 --> 00:03:54,470
[1949 film] Well, thanks so much.

78
00:03:54,870 --> 00:03:55,970
[1949 film] I had a nice time.

79
00:03:56,670 --> 00:03:57,230
[1949 film] So did I.

80
00:03:57,790 --> 00:03:58,450
[1949 film] Good night, Woody.

81
00:03:59,030 --> 00:03:59,490
[1949 film] Night, Ann.

82
00:04:00,550 --> 00:04:04,310
So what does this film reveal about America in 1949?

83
00:04:04,830 --> 00:04:06,970
A country just out of a war wanted order,

84
00:04:07,010 --> 00:04:10,130
and it wanted it early: marriage in your early twenties,

85
00:04:10,170 --> 00:04:13,310
after a courtship run by rules everyone could recite.

86
00:04:13,670 --> 00:04:16,970
The year before, Alfred Kinsey's report on male sexual

87
00:04:17,010 --> 00:04:20,490
behavior had become a best seller by documenting how far

88
00:04:20,530 --> 00:04:22,850
real behavior strayed from the script.

89
00:04:23,190 --> 00:04:26,090
Today the median American first marries at around thirty

90
00:04:26,130 --> 00:04:28,470
for men and twenty-eight for women.

91
00:04:28,770 --> 00:04:31,790
The film itself survives in the public domain,

92
00:04:31,830 --> 00:04:36,410
because no renewal of its 1949 copyright has ever been

93
00:04:36,450 --> 00:04:39,690
found. A reminder that the good night at the door was

94
00:04:39,730 --> 00:04:41,690
never only about manners.

95
00:04:41,850 --> 00:04:43,750
It was about who got to decide.

